# ai-specs:kit version={{VERSION}} prefix={{PREFIX}}
# Managed by ai-specs (`ai_specs.py init`). Change the kit and re-run init instead of editing this file.
schema: spec-driven

context: |
  {{PROJECT}} keeps what it does today as OpenSpec specs (openspec/specs) and every change as an OpenSpec change
  (openspec/changes/<id>) following the ai-specs standard (docs/standard.md in the ai-specs repo).
  Artifacts are written in {{LANGUAGE}}; the headers and markers OpenSpec parses keep their canonical English syntax.
  OpenSpec keeps intent, requirements, design and tasks. Operational state (owner, status, approvals, run logs)
  lives in the tracker or orchestrator, never in these files.
  Requirement ids use the prefix {{PREFIX}}- (for example {{PREFIX}}-001) and never change once archived.
  Every change declares a size: S (one sitting, one repo), M (several task groups or a contract/schema change),
  L (several repos, data migration, security-sensitive or epic). The size decides which artifacts are required.

rules:
  proposal:
    - 'Start with the line "**Size:** S", "**Size:** M" or "**Size:** L".'
    - 'Sections Why and What Changes (with a concrete "Excludes" list), and always a "Not to add" section listing what an agent might add unasked.'
    - 'For M and L add "Prior art and reuse" (reuse / extend / build new, and why; new dependencies are proposals for the owner).'
    - 'For M add a "Change impact" table (changed thing, used by today, breaks?, mitigation/rollback); for L it goes in design.md.'
    - 'Never reserve a version and never record status, owners or approvals here.'
  specs:
    - 'One observable behaviour per Requirement using SHALL or MUST, titled "{{PREFIX}}-NNN — name"; keep existing ids.'
    - 'At least one Scenario per Requirement with GIVEN, WHEN and THEN, where THEN states an exact reference result.'
    - 'End every Scenario with a "- **Proof:**" line naming the command or test that demonstrates it and what counts as a pass.'
    - 'Link OpenAPI or JSON Schema contracts instead of duplicating them in Markdown.'
  design:
    - 'Required for size L. Explain decisions and rejected alternatives, compatibility, migration, security, operations and test strategy.'
    - 'Include the Change impact table and a rollback plan (revert plus any data or migration step).'
  tasks:
    - 'Checkboxes only, grouped in numbered sections, kept current during implementation.'
    - 'Each task is a vertical slice in one repo that fits a fresh agent context (about 400 changed lines and 8 files at most).'
    - 'Format every task as "- [ ] N.M <what> — test first: <test or n/a> — proof: `<command>`".'
    - 'Order baseline, contracts (provider before consumer), behaviour, docs and removal of replaced sources; mark sensitive-path tasks "(human gate)".'
